    shape pv0 #b5482e shape circle x 110 y 250 radius 9 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    shape pv1 #b5482e shape circle x 370 y 250 radius 9 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    shape pv2 #b5482e shape circle x 490 y 350 radius 9 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    shape pv3 #b5482e shape circle x 230 y 350 radius 9 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    shape pv4 #b5482e shape circle x 300 y 620 radius 9 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    group pdots members ["pv0","pv1","pv2","pv3","pv4"] hidden true

    card title "Corners − edges + faces = ?"

Take a cube. Count its corners, take away its edges, and add its faces. What do you get?
    draw cube

    ask "For a cube: corners minus edges plus faces. Type the number you get." answer "2"
        right: Two: eight corners, minus twelve edges, plus six faces.
        wrong: Count the hidden corner at the back too.

    draw corners tv

Eight corners, twelve edges,
    draw te

and six faces: the front, the back, and four around the sides.
    draw face tf

    erase face

So eight minus twelve plus six makes two.
    draw sum_c

    hide cube corners tv te tf sum_c

Now a pyramid with a square base: five corners, eight edges, five faces.
    draw pyr pdots

Two again. And it works for every solid without holes, however many sides it has.
    draw sum_p

A football's panels: sixty corners, ninety edges, thirty-two faces. Two.
    draw ball

Leonhard Euler spotted the rule in 1750, and wrote about it to his friend Christian Goldbach.
    draw eul

    hide pyr pdots sum_p ball eul

But drill a hole through a solid, like this picture frame,
    draw frame

and the answer drops to zero.
    draw zero

So the number keeps track of holes, and that idea grew into a whole field of maths: topology.
    draw topo
---
